---
title: Remote access
description: Use KoboldCpp from your phone or another PC - on the same network with the LAN IP, or over the internet with Remote Tunnel. Set a password first.
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

KoboldCpp runs on one PC, and you can use it from other devices. KoboldAI Lite works in any browser, including on phones.

| Where the other device is | Use |
|---|---|
| Same Wi-Fi or network | [The PC's network address](#same-network) |
| Anywhere | [Remote Tunnel](#anywhere-remote-tunnel) |

{% aside type="caution" %}
Set a **Password:** on the **Network** tab (`--password`) before you open KoboldCpp to other devices. See [Passwords and security](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/).
{% /aside %}

## Same network

KoboldCpp listens on all network interfaces by default, so devices on your network can already reach it.

{% steps %}

1. If others share your network, set a **Password:** on the **Network** tab. Leave **Host:** on the same tab empty; with `127.0.0.1`, only this PC can connect.
2. Launch KoboldCpp.
3. Find the PC's IP address on your network, for example `192.168.1.20`.
   - Windows: run `ipconfig` in Command Prompt and look for **IPv4 Address**.
   - Linux: run `hostname -I`.
   - macOS: run `ipconfig getifaddr en0`.
4. On the other device, open `http://<IP address>:5001`, for example `http://192.168.1.20:5001`.

{% /steps %}

The console still prints `http://localhost:5001`. That address is only for the PC itself.

If the page does not load, the PC's firewall is usually blocking port 5001, or the IP address is wrong:

- **Windows:** on first launch, Windows may ask whether KoboldCpp may use the network. Allow **Private networks**. If you missed it, open **Windows Security** > **Firewall & network protection** > **Allow an app through firewall** and tick KoboldCpp for **Private**.
- **Linux (ufw):** `sudo ufw allow 5001/tcp`.

Browsers allow the microphone and camera only on HTTPS or on the PC itself (`localhost`). For voice input or the camera from another device, use the **Remote Tunnel** link or [HTTPS](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/#https-ssl).

For other apps, use the same address in place of `localhost`, for example `http://192.168.1.20:5001/v1` for OpenAI-compatible apps.

## Anywhere: Remote Tunnel

**Remote Tunnel** creates a public HTTPS link through Cloudflare. It works even behind a firewall.

{% steps %}

1. Set a **Password:** on the **Network** tab.
2. Tick **Remote Tunnel** on the **Quick Launch** or **Network** tab. On the command line, add `--remotetunnel`.
3. Launch. The first time, KoboldCpp downloads Cloudflare's `cloudflared` program from GitHub.
4. Wait for the link in the console:

   ```text {% title="Console" frame="terminal" %}
   Your remote tunnel is ready, please connect to https://<random-words>.trycloudflare.com
   ```

5. Open the link on any device to use KoboldAI Lite. For other apps, add `/api` or `/v1` to it, as printed in the console.

{% /steps %}

The link changes every time you launch KoboldCpp.

Anyone who has the link can reach your KoboldCpp. The password keeps them out of text generation, but not out of image generation. See [what the password protects](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/#what-the-password-protects).

If the tunnel fails, the console prints `Error: Could not create cloudflare tunnel!` or `Remote Tunnel Failed!`. KoboldCpp needs internet access to download `cloudflared` and to open the tunnel.

Remote Tunnel does not start in terminal-only modes (`--cli`, `--prompt`, `--benchmark`).

## Share with the world: AI Horde

To let anyone use your model through the AI Horde network instead of a private link, run KoboldCpp as a Horde worker. See [Horde worker](/docs/features/horde-worker/).

## Advanced: choose the network address

**Host:** (`--host`) sets the address KoboldCpp listens on. Leave it empty for all interfaces, use `127.0.0.1` for this PC only, or one of the PC's IP addresses to listen on that network only. When **Host:** is set, the console prints that address in place of `localhost`.

The port is **Port:** on the **Network** tab (`--port`, default 5001).

## Advanced: HTTPS on your network

On your own network, KoboldCpp serves plain HTTP. To serve HTTPS, set **SSL Cert:** and **SSL Key:** (`--ssl cert.pem key.pem`). The console addresses then start with `https://`. See [HTTPS (SSL)](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/#https-ssl).

With SSL on, **Remote Tunnel** connects to KoboldCpp over HTTPS and accepts its certificate even if it is self-signed.

## Safety checklist

- Set a **Password:** before you use **Remote Tunnel** or share on your network.
- If admin mode is on, set an **Admin Password:**.
- Image generation and `/tts_to_audio` are open to anyone who can reach the server. Load those models only on instances you trust, or limit image size with `--sdclamped`.
- For shared servers, consider `--ratelimit`, `--genlimit` and `--maxrequestsize`. See [Limits for shared servers](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/#limits-for-shared-servers).

